Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.50,0:00:07.63,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}DRAGONN is a randomized hashing algorithm for gradient sparsification in data-parallel distributed training,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:07.71,0:00:09.83,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}from Rice University and ThirdAI.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.91,0:00:11.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}In distributed training,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.51,0:00:16.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}synchronizing gradients across GPUs is the main efficiency bottleneck,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.19,0:00:19.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and gradient sparsification methods were meant to help,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.86,0:00:24.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}but their own compression overhead has become the new bottleneck.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.20,0:00:30.79,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}DRAGONN replaces the exact parallel-prefix-sum operations used by prior methods with direct hashing,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:30.87,0:00:36.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}cutting compression time by up to seventy percent and delivering up to three-and-a-half
Dialogue: 0,0:00:36.68,0:00:38.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}times faster training throughput.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:39.18,0:00:45.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Data-parallel distributed training is the standard way to scale deep learning across many GPUs,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.47,0:00:49.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}but synchronizing gradients between workers is the dominant cost.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.77,0:00:55.58,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Gradient sparsification promised relief by transmitting only a small subset of gradients,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:55.66,0:01:01.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}yet in practice the time spent compressing the gradients grew so large that it cancelled
Dialogue: 0,0:01:01.48,0:01:03.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}out the communication savings.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:03.47,0:01:07.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The paper shows that once a tensor exceeds about sixteen megabytes,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.90,0:01:12.79,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}this compression overhead becomes the single largest efficiency bottleneck,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:12.87,0:01:18.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and for small tensors sparsification can even be slower than sending everything.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:18.46,0:01:22.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The leading sparsification method, Deep Gradient Compression,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:22.64,0:01:28.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}selects gradients above an estimated threshold, which is an approximate operation.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:28.25,0:01:34.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}But to place those gradients into memory without conflicts it relies on parallel prefix sum,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:34.55,0:01:38.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}an exact algorithm that builds a balanced binary tree,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:38.25,0:01:40.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}runs in logarithmic sequential steps,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.78,0:01:45.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and touches memory about seven times more than the theoretical lower bound.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:45.92,0:01:53.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The authors call this an exact-approximate mismatch: since only an approximate set of top gradients is required,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:53.59,0:01:58.03,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}using an expensive exact algorithm to place them is wasted effort.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:58.41,0:02:01.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The paper makes three contributions.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:02.00,0:02:05.51,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}First, it proposes DRAGONN,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:05.59,0:02:09.10,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}a hashing-based sparsification algorithm that slashes compression overhead while keeping
Dialogue: 0,0:02:09.19,0:02:12.70,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}the same per-iteration convergence,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.78,0:02:16.29,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}backed by theoretical bounds on both compression error and generalization error.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.37,0:02:19.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Second,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.96,0:02:23.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it adds two system-level optimizations: an efficiency-aware tensor selection that only
Dialogue: 0,0:02:23.55,0:02:27.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}compresses tensors where it actually pays off,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:27.14,0:02:30.66,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and a sparse decoding scheme that keeps decode cost from growing with the number of GPUs.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:30.74,0:02:34.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Third,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:34.33,0:02:37.84,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it evaluates the method broadly across vision and recommendation models and demonstrates
Dialogue: 0,0:02:37.92,0:02:41.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}substantial end-to-end training speedups.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:41.81,0:02:47.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}DRAGONN works by pre-allocating a small memory buffer sized to the compression ratio.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:47.65,0:02:51.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}For each gradient whose absolute value clears the threshold,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:51.77,0:02:56.09,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it hashes the gradient's index to a slot and writes it directly,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.17,0:02:59.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}rather than scanning to compute nonzero positions.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:59.61,0:03:04.20,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}If two indices collide, the later one simply overwrites the earlier,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:04.28,0:03:06.75,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and any slot left empty maps to zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:06.83,0:03:10.46,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Because writing memory is an atomic operation on GPUs,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:10.54,0:03:16.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}many threads can hash and write at the same time with no dependency between them,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.10,0:03:21.04,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so the whole compression needs only d comparisons plus l hash operations,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.12,0:03:23.79,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}essentially the theoretical lower bound.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:23.87,0:03:25.03,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}To deploy it well,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:25.11,0:03:31.14,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}DRAGONN adds two tricks: it only compresses a tensor when the compression time is smaller
Dialogue: 0,0:03:31.22,0:03:33.68,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}than the communication it would save,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:33.76,0:03:40.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and it batches all the compressed tensors received from other workers into one sparse decode,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:40.16,0:03:45.23,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}so decoding cost stays nearly constant no matter how many GPUs participate.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:45.61,0:03:52.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The experiments span four models covering both vision and recommendation-style workloads:
Dialogue: 0,0:03:52.58,0:03:54.77,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}ResNet50 trained on ImageNet,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:54.85,0:04:01.43,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Vision Transformer and MLP-Mixer fine-tuned on Cifar10 from ImageNet-21k pretraining,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:01.51,0:04:07.22,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and an extreme multi-label classification model on the Wiki10-31K dataset.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:07.30,0:04:15.29,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The testbed is sixteen Nvidia V100 GPUs across two machines connected by a twenty-five gigabit network,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:15.37,0:04:18.26,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}running PyTorch with Horovod and NCCL,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:18.34,0:04:25.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and using memory-momentum error feedback to preserve accuracy across all sparsification methods.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.16,0:04:32.17,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The headline result is that DRAGONN cuts compression time by up to seventy percent compared
Dialogue: 0,0:04:32.25,0:04:35.12,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}to the best existing sparsification methods.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:35.20,0:04:37.73,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Reaching the same level of convergence,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:37.81,0:04:44.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it delivers up to three-and-a-half times higher total training throughput than Deep Gradient Compression,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:44.84,0:04:49.38,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and up to nearly thirty-six times over full gradient synchronization,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:49.46,0:04:53.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}all while matching the test accuracy of full synchronization.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:53.55,0:04:57.82,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Importantly, its advantage over DGC grows as more GPUs are added,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:57.90,0:05:00.03,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}which signals strong scalability,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:00.11,0:05:04.65,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and the authors expect the gains to widen further on faster networks.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:05.03,0:05:09.77,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}An ablation study separates the contributions of the three components.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:09.85,0:05:14.52,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The hashing-based compressor by itself provides the base improvement;
Dialogue: 0,0:05:14.60,0:05:19.20,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}layering on efficiency-aware tensor selection increases the speedup,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:19.28,0:05:22.98,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}for example to about one-point-nine times on one model,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:23.06,0:05:27.87,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and adding sparse decoding pushes it as high as four-point-three times.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:27.95,0:05:32.90,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Notably, the benefit of sparse decoding can exceed the number of workers,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:32.98,0:05:39.23,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}because it eliminates the decode cost that otherwise grows linearly with the number of GPUs.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:39.31,0:05:45.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}Micro-benchmarks confirm DRAGONN has the lowest encoding time across all tensor sizes
Dialogue: 0,0:05:45.16,0:05:49.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and that its decode time stays nearly flat as GPUs are added.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:49.66,0:05:57.45,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The key numbers are: up to a seventy percent reduction in compression time versus state-of-the-art sparsification;
Dialogue: 0,0:05:57.53,0:06:03.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}up to three-and-a-half times faster total training than DGC and up to thirty-six times
Dialogue: 0,0:06:03.47,0:06:07.13,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}faster than full synchronization at equal convergence;
Dialogue: 0,0:06:07.21,0:06:11.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}per-model speedups over DGC of about one-point-four on ResNet50,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:11.63,0:06:15.07,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}two-point-two on ViT, one-point-seven on MLP-Mixer,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:15.15,0:06:18.32,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and three-point-five on extreme classification;
Dialogue: 0,0:06:18.40,0:06:24.67,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}and a decoding overhead reduced from growing linearly with the GPU count to nearly constant.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:25.05,0:06:32.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}The lasting lesson is simple: since gradient sparsification only ever needs an approximate set of top gradients,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.68,0:06:36.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}it should not pay for an exact selection algorithm.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:36.16,0:06:40.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}By swapping exact parallel prefix sum for direct randomized hashing,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:40.80,0:06:42.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}DRAGONN turns a sequential,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:42.64,0:06:47.33,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}dependency-heavy compression step into an embarrassingly parallel one,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:47.41,0:06:52.85,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}removes the overhead that had been cancelling out sparsification's benefits, and,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:52.93,0:06:56.32,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}together with tensor selection and sparse decoding,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:56.40,0:07:02.05,Default,,0,0,0,,{\c&H00FFFFFF&}makes gradient sparsification finally pay off at scale without sacrificing accuracy.
